      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To our knowledge, there is no prospective study to date about the prognostic factors of dorsal foraminotomy. The aim of this prospective study was to provide further information in this field."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "From January 1994 to January 1995, we performed a prospective, consecutive study of 54 patients, each of whose lateral herniated cervical disc was operated on via a dorsal foraminotomy. We analyzed the general data, the case history, the neurological examination at admission, and all data from imaging examinations and therapy. Most of the patients (93%) were followed up at 1 year, postoperatively. The patients were divided into one group with good results and another group with bad results, according to their ratings on a pain scale. The groups were analyzed in relation to the patients' initial condition."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At follow-up, 94% of the patients had completely recovered or their condition had improved."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "A long duration of preoperative complaints and a long-standing neurological deficit seem to be important prognostic factors for a bad outcome after dorsal foraminotomy."
        }
      ]},
